<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/manchester-mills.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/manchester-mills-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/manchester-mills.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Manchester</span></nav><p class="kicker">Merrimack Valley · Hillsborough County</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Manchester, NH</h1><p class="lede">Manchester’s housing was built for mill workers stacked three floors high, and its claims still arrive that way – one loss, several units, several opinions about the number. An independent appraiser keeps the argument about money, not mood.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Hillsborough County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Merrimack Valley</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>triple-deckers, brick mill conversions and post-war capes</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> North End, West Side, the Millyard, Rimmon Heights, Hallsville, the South End.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> The December 2008 ice storm brought down limbs and lines across Hillsborough County, and Manchester neighborhoods spent days sorting out roof, siding and spoiled-food claims afterward.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>Burst pipes</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Microbursts</li><li>Kitchen &amp; heating fires</li><li>Falling limbs</li></ul></div>

<h2>A city of stacked floors and shared walls</h2>
<p>Manchester grew up around the Amoskeag mills on the Merrimack River, and the housing followed the payroll. On the West Side, in Rimmon Heights and through the streets east of Elm, you still find wood-frame triple-deckers and two-families built a century or more ago, many now split between an owner-occupant and tenants. Up in the North End the lots get bigger and the houses get older-money: Colonial Revivals, foursquares and the occasional brick Tudor. Across the river and out toward the city line, post-war capes and ranches fill in the gaps.</p>
<p>That mix matters when a loss lands. A pipe that lets go on the third floor of a triple-decker does not stay on the third floor. By the time the water finds the basement, you have three ceilings, three kitchens and possibly a tenant’s belongings in play – and an insurer’s estimate that may price only the unit where the leak started.</p>
<h2>Where Manchester estimates tend to come up short</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Plaster and lath.</strong> Older interiors are often scoped as half-inch drywall. Matching or properly replacing plaster costs more, and the gap adds up fast across several rooms.</li>
<li><strong>Vertical water losses.</strong> Drying, demolition and rebuild on every floor the water touched, not just the one with the broken fitting.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of rents.</strong> Owner-occupied multifamily buildings may carry fair rental value coverage; check your policy before you assume it isn’t there.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades.</strong> Rebuilding stairs, egress and electrical in an old multifamily can trigger requirements the first estimate ignores. Ordinance-or-law coverage, if you have it, is worth reading closely.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/pipe-burst-water-appraisal/">burst-pipe and water appraisal</a> work starts with the path of the water, floor by floor.</p>
<h2>The Millyard and other condo questions</h2>
<p>The brick mill buildings along Commercial Street have been reworked into offices, labs and residences. Condo and loft owners there face the usual master-policy puzzle: what the association insures, what your own unit policy picks up, and who pays the deductible. When an association and its carrier disagree on the amount of a building loss, appraisal is often an option. See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">commercial and HOA appraisal</a> for how that works.</p>
<h2>Summer wind, winter ice</h2>
<p>Manchester sits in the part of the state where July thunderstorms and microbursts do their work, snapping old maples onto porches and peeling shingles off roofs. Then winter brings the reverse problem: snow that sits on low-slope rear additions and ice dams that push meltwater into wall cavities. If your carrier says the roof was simply old, or that interior staining was long-term seepage, the disagreement may be about scope and price rather than coverage – which is exactly what the appraisal clause is built to settle.</p>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser works across Manchester, from Hallsville and the South End to the North End. If you are weighing whether to demand appraisal, start with <a href="/how-nh-appraisal-works/">how New Hampshire appraisal works</a> or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">get in touch</a>.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in Manchester</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in Manchester and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by Manchester owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>I own a three-family in Manchester and live on one floor. Does appraisal cover the whole building?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If the whole building is insured under your policy and the dispute is over the amount of loss, appraisal can address every unit the damage reached. Tenants’ own belongings are usually covered by their own renters policies, not yours.</p></div></details><details><summary>My insurer priced my plaster walls as drywall. Is that an appraisal issue?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often, yes. Whether the repair method and price restore what was there is an amount-of-loss question. Bring photos of the existing walls and, if you can, a contractor’s plaster quote.</p></div></details></div>
